/* DISCOVERY (space-time folds): Result 51 showed the delayed-network covariance is separable only
   at delta = 0, and Result 52 designed folds on the SPACE axis alone. Plan 24's P2.5 asks what the
   two-axis problem costs. It is not intractable: the covariance is a sum of KRONECKER terms whose
   count is bounded by the SPILLOVER TRUNCATION -- never by the graph diameter or the panel length --
   and the count drops further on a graph whose shell operators commute. Everything below is exact.
   proofs/space_time_folds.v machine-checks the fold-sandwich algebra and the weight comparison. */
display2d : false$

/* The panel covariance, from the generative law
     x_i(t) = sum_d g_d sum_{j in shell d(i)} z_j(t - delta*d),  Cov(z_j(t), z_k(s)) = 1{j=k} ph^|t-s|
   is  Sigma_{(i,t),(k,s)} = sum_{d,e} g_d g_e [S_d S_e]_{ik} ph^|(t-s) - delta*(d-e)|,
   i.e.  Sigma = sum_{q=-D}^{D} P_q (x) T_q  with  P_q = sum_{d-e=q} g_d g_e S_d S_e  and
   [T_q]_{ts} = ph^|(t-s) - delta*q|.  Verified against 2*10^5 simulated panels to 0.021 on a scale
   of 2.3 (chc.regret.space_time_fold_certificate re-runs it). */

/* (1) THE INDEX PAIRING. P_{-q} = P_q^T and T_{-q} = T_q^T, because swapping (d,e) transposes the
   spatial factor and negates the temporal shift. Checked on the exponent, which is where it lives. */
print("(1) |(s-t) - delta*(-q)| - |(t-s) - delta*q| =",
      ratsimp( abs((s - t) - delta*(-q)) - abs((t - s) - delta*q) ), "  (=0: T_{-q} = T_q^T)")$

/* (2) EACH +-q PAIR CONTRIBUTES AT MOST TWO KRONECKER TERMS, and exactly one when P_q is symmetric.
   The identity  P (x) T + P^T (x) T^T = 2*(sym(P) (x) sym(T) + skew(P) (x) skew(T))  splits the
   pair into a symmetric and an antisymmetric channel; the second vanishes iff P = P^T. Checked
   entrywise on generic 2x2 blocks -- the statement is bilinear, so 2x2 is not a special case. */
P : matrix([p11, p12], [p21, p22])$
T : matrix([t11, t12], [t21, t22])$
symp : (P + transpose(P))/2$  skewp : (P - transpose(P))/2$
symt : (T + transpose(T))/2$  skewt : (T - transpose(T))/2$
lhs2 : kronecker_product(P, T) + kronecker_product(transpose(P), transpose(T))$
rhs2 : 2*( kronecker_product(symp, symt) + kronecker_product(skewp, skewt) )$
print("(2) P(x)T + P^T(x)T^T - 2(sym(x)sym + skew(x)skew) =",
      ratsimp( lhs2 - rhs2 ), "  (=0)")$
print("    skew(P) = 0 kills the second channel:  skew(P) at p21=p12 =",
      ratsimp( subst(p21 = p12, skewp) ), "  (=0)")$

/* (3) THE TWO EXTREME SHIFTS ARE ALWAYS SYMMETRIC, on ANY graph. q = 0 gives sum_d g_d^2 S_d^2 and
   q = D gives g_D g_0 S_D S_0 = g_D g_0 S_D -- a single term with the identity, so it cannot fail to
   be symmetric. Only q = 1 .. D-1 can carry an antisymmetric part, and only when the shell
   operators fail to commute. Hence  rank <= (D+1) + (D-1) = 2D, and never 2D+1. */
print("(3) shifts q = -D..D count 2*D+1; symmetric-only channels D+1; extra channels D-1")$
print("    bound  (D+1) + (D-1) - 2*D =", ratsimp( (D + 1) + (D - 1) - 2*D ), "  (=0)")$
/* Commuting shells (vertex-transitive graphs: cycle, torus, any Cayley graph) make every P_q
   symmetric, because P_q^T = sum_{d-e=q} g_d g_e S_e S_d = P_q. Then the rank is D+1.
   MEASURED, cycle m=14: 2, 3, 4, 5 at D = 1, 2, 3, 4.  Path / Erdos-Renyi: 2, 4, 6, 8 = 2D. */

/* (4) SEPARABILITY IS EXACTLY delta = 0, recovering Result 51's STEP 7 from the Kronecker side.
   All the shifts collapse onto T_0, so Sigma = (sum_q P_q) (x) T_0 and the rank is 1. */
print("(4) shift at delta=0 :  subst(delta=0, |(t-s) - delta*q|) - |t-s| =",
      ratsimp( subst(delta = 0, abs((t - s) - delta*q)) - abs(t - s) ), "  (=0)")$

/* (5) THE FOLD OPERATOR'S SQUARE, which is what makes the two-axis design a MIN-WEIGHT BALANCED CUT
   on the product set. With E the grand-mean projector, F the within-fold projector, E^2=E, F^2=F,
   EF=FE=E, the algebra <I,E,F> is closed, so an operator is a coefficient TRIPLE and multiplication
   is a rule on triples. A_u = (1, -r^2, r^2-1). */
mul(u, v) := block([a1:u[1], b1:u[2], c1:u[3], a2:v[1], b2:v[2], c2:v[3]],
  [ a1*a2,
    a1*b2 + b1*a2 + b1*b2 + b1*c2 + c1*b2,
    a1*c2 + c1*a2 + c1*c2 ])$
au   : [1, -r^2, r^2 - 1]$
sq   : mul(au, au)$
print("(5) A_u^2 coefficients =", ratsimp(sq))$
print("    residual vs (1, -r^4, r^4-1) =", ratsimp( sq - [1, -r^4, r^4 - 1] ), "  (=0)")$
/* So tr(A_u^2 Sigma) = tr(Sigma) - r^4 (1'Sigma 1)/N + (r^4-1)(K/N) sum_f 1_f' Sigma 1_f, and only
   the last term sees the partition, with a POSITIVE weight for K >= 2 (r = K/(K-1) > 1). */
print("    partition weight  (r^4-1) at r=K/(K-1) =", factor( ratsimp( subst(r = K/(K-1), r^4 - 1) ) ),
      "  (>0 for K>=2)")$

/* (6) THE TIME AXIS RE-WEIGHTS THE SPACE PROBLEM. A fold held CONSTANT in time scores
   sum_{t,s} [T_q]_{ts} = w_q, not the single-slice value [T_q]_{tt} = ph^(delta*|q|) that Result 52
   uses. The two are NOT proportional, so the panel's one-axis design is not the cross-section's. */
w(q, ph, delta, p) := sum( sum( ph^abs((t - s) - delta*q), s, 0, p - 1), t, 0, p - 1)$
print("(6) w_0 at p=3 =", ratsimp( w(0, ph, delta, 3) ))$
print("    w_1 at p=3, delta=1 =", ratsimp( w(1, ph, 1, 3) ))$
print("    proportionality residual  w_1/w_0 - ph^delta  at p=3, delta=1 =",
      ratsimp( w(1, ph, 1, 3)/w(0, ph, 1, 3) - ph ),
      "  (NOT identically 0: the panel weight is not the slice weight)")$
print("    at ph=3/5 the two weights are", ratsimp( subst(ph = 3/5, w(1, ph, 1, 3)/w(0, ph, 1, 3)) ),
      "vs", 3/5)$
print("    the residual factors as", factor( ratsimp( w(1, ph, 1, 3)/w(0, ph, 1, 3) - ph ) ),
      "  (vanishes only at ph = +-1: the degenerate limits, never in between)")$

/* (7) THE SHIFT LEAVES THE WINDOW, which is why cutting time pays MORE as the delay grows. Every
   temporal lag in the panel obeys |t-s| <= p-1, so once the shift delta*q exceeds that, EVERY entry
   of T_q is at most ph^(delta*q-(p-1)) and the whole block is exponentially small in the excess.
   The design then has almost no reason to keep those pairs together, and the freed budget goes to
   the space axis -- measured as 0.0-1.2% of the objective at delta=1 rising to 12.9-19.2% at
   delta=4 (m=8, p=4, K=2, cycle), with the heuristic matching exhaustive enumeration exactly. */
assume(delta*q - p + 1 >= 0)$
print("(7) worst-case exponent gap:  min over |tau|<=p-1 of  |tau - delta*q| - (delta*q - (p-1)) =",
      ratsimp( abs((p - 1) - delta*q) - (delta*q - (p - 1)) ),
      "  (=0 at tau = p-1, so the bound ph^(delta*q-(p-1)) is ATTAINED, not merely valid)")$
print("    mass bound  w_q <= p^2 ph^(delta*q-(p-1))  is that entry bound summed over p^2 pairs")$
